      "text": "In all such partly verbal, partly nonverbal kinds of rhetorical devices, the nonverbal element also persuades by reason of its symbolic character. Paper need not know the meaning of fire in order to burn. But in the \"idea\" offire there is a persuasive ingredient. By this route something of the rhetorical motive comes to lurk in every \"meaning\" however purely \"scientific\" its pretensions. Wherever there is persuasion, there is rhetoric. And wherever there is \"meaning,\" there is \"persuasion.\" Food, eaten and digested, is not rhetorical. But in the meaning o[food there is much rhetoric, the meaning being persuasive enough for the idea of food to be used, like the ideas ofreligion, as a rhetorical device of statesmen. (RM 172-73)\n\nThis passage seems far from clear. Additionally, the numerous ironizing quotation marks and italics indicate various forms of pressure and stress. The word meaning, not insignificantly, is italicized twice and twice placed in quotation marks. What does meaning mean here? The most direct reading would be that Burke understands meaning to emerge as our ideas about the world, making meaning the sense or significance of things, events, and relations. Meaning is gleaned from the symbolicity that drapes over (or maybe permeates) the mundane world, circulating it as the significance available to us. The mention of science is important here, for Burke is clearly dissatisfied with a positivist, scientific claim of mere facticity for things of the world, as if measuring qualia sufficed for generating meaning. Thus, he will grant that paper, being paper, has no ideas as such; its rapid oxidation at temperatures around 451 degrees Fahrenheit is a purely chemical reaction. But to remain at this level is to willfully (or methodologically) remain at observation degree zero, and it obscures fire's role in human affairs. When Bradbury's Fireman comes to\n\nburn the books, though, we move into a different realm, one of meaning, and we see that fire is a full rhetorical participant. Fire, then, is more than just a sensed material phenomenon; its integration into the human world necessitates ideas. These ideas mean, and insofar as they mean, they bodily invoke, and insofar as they bodily invoke, they persuade.\n\nAs I observed in my discussion of Hawhee, this is not an abstract notion of fire as meaningful; rather, it is one entirely grounded in the bodily integration of ideas stemming from physical experience. As Burke puts it in Attitudes toward History, \"our primeval ancestors, by learning language, no longer experienced a sensation solely as a sensation\" (382). As he goes on to explain, we may like the warmth ofa fire, and such warmth is certainly a sensation, but as soon as we start putting such experience into language, the words come to \"tell a story\"; as a result, attitudes (or affective stances) can emerge and be deployed across various forms of discursive interaction, such as the movement from \"that feels warm\" to the observation that someone is \"warm-hearted\" (Burke,AH383).\n\nThis elevation of meaning over mundane, objective matter is further evinced in other passages. Later in A Rhetoric of Motives Burke again discusses the effect of material phenomena. This time he calls them a form of intuition, and he then tethers intuition to a notion of infancy. One can perceive a seashell, for instance, and note the generative regularity of its spiral. but without more firmly and robustly grasping the intelligibility of that spiral in the world of human meaning, such as reasoning out the mathematical functions that generate various spirals, such an encounter remains stuck in what he terms \"infancy,\" a notion he further compares to the \"realm of the unconscious in dreams\" (RM 174-75). But before we begin complaining about the terribly slanted and problematic valuation here, where conscious reason trumps infantile intuition, we must see the antistrophe he proposes immediately thereafter. If a poem brings up springtime, Burke explains, we must understand the locution beyond the \"positivist's 'fact'\" of spring (175). Burke's explanation for this is quote worthy:\n\nAn \"intuition\" of spring is not a mere passive perception, a datum of sensory (or even supersensory) knowledge. It is an acting-with, as our \"intuitions\" of a phoebe's song in spring are not merely the sensory perception of the air in vibration, nor even a sheerly physical response to the return of spring which the song may signalize, but an actingwith a wider orbit of meanings, some of them not intrinsically \"springlike\" at all. The \"mystery\" here centers in the fact that the articulate",
