    {
      "raw_rank": 7,
      "chunk_id": "ab2442b48417ead0:00126",
      "distance": 0.7970286011695862,
      "path_rel": "rhetorical_ontology/Rickert, Thomas - Ambient Rhetoric- The Attunements of Rhetorical Being_(2013)_[My Copy].pdf",
      "page_start": 213,
      "page_end": 213,
      "text": "I want to pursue a different path, one suggested by Heidegger's nutshell statement in Parmenides that \"only when man becomes the subject do non-human beings become objects\" (165). Language emerges as it is because it is already entangled with the material realm. making the subject/object problem derivative of an ambient coconstitutiveness. In the previous chapter I considered this from the perspective oflanguage; here, I will emphasize things, in particular by looking into the work of Graham Harman, whose reading of the tool section of Heidegger's Being and Time\n\nhas resulted in a theory Harman calls \"speculative realism,\" or sometimes \"object-oriented philosophy.\" As I discussed in the introduction, speculative realism reflects a fresh approach to understanding what a material object is or can be. I will also attempt to integrate this into the ambient perspective I am proposing, suggesting that objects both relate to as well as withdraw from their environments. Such a perspective seeks to place rhetoric in the richest possible understanding of materiality. In doing so, I hope to further the argument that rhetoric is not solely human doing, for it is conditioned by, and hence responsive to the directives of, material things. This thought returns us to the theme, broached via Diane Davis in the previous chapter, of an originary affectability or conditionality and a notion ofpersuadability that has a material dimension. The challenge will be to make this argument without falling back into na'ive realism, material determinism, or some new permutation of realism or idealism. A further challenge will lie in showing rhetorical theory to need a notion of materialism that includes meaning but declines to stop where meaning is understood to break off.\n\nI argue, in other words, that human being requires an ontological weddedness to things and environs that affords rhetoricity as a modality of being-together but does not thereby exhaust rhetoric, since it cannot be relegated exclusively to human symbolicity and doing. We cannot, I think, be satisfied with Judith Butler's classic postmodern statement that every reference to a body is a further construction of that body (10). That again sneaks in the very human-centeredness that postmodern thought largely sought to supersede. Butler may actively disclaim that there is an \"I\" who controls discourse, but in the end it is discourse that transcends and trumps materiality (Butler 7). It might be better to say, in a distinctly Heideggerian idiom, that every bodying of the body is a further formation of our being-in-the-world, with the understanding that the bodying of the body is already an event attuned to and conditioned by the world, an event that includes the material body itself as agent (Heidegger, Z 97). 5 But note how this differs from Butler's claim: the bodying of the body works from a permeating sense of world, one that in crucial respects dissolves some of the boundaries between where we say body ends and environs begin. Such a perspective allows cultural significance and meaning to emerge as one way of engaging and explaining who we are and what the world is, but not the final instance; nor is there any lingering trace of the separation of \"culture\" from \"nature.\" There is no world \"out there\" fundamentally distinct from the body and over which humans layer meaning, because there are no\n\nhuman beings absolved from the world who could do such a thing. The things of the world are already integral to what we mean by human being, making human being a larger, shifting composite of engaged perception, interlocution, and activity. Such a matrix of engagement brings the world to reveal itself in various ways that never exhaust what is there, that never fully wrest from concealment what withdraws. Nor, finally, does such a matrix allow for things to wrest themselves from the mutually conditioning forms ofrelationality that constitute any object whatsoever, including human beings. As I have previously discussed, Diane Davis devotes her Inessential Solidarity to this position, although she emphasizes a presymbolic affectability and responsibility to the social other. In a similar way, I seek here to explore how \"having\" a world, in Heidegger's idiom, requires a certain sense of thingness from which world-having emerges (Heidegger FCM177). 6\n\nHow to Have Fun with Hammers: Graham Harman's Tool-being",
