    {
      "raw_rank": 8,
      "chunk_id": "ab2442b48417ead0:00169",
      "distance": 0.8099597692489624,
      "path_rel": "rhetorical_ontology/Rickert, Thomas - Ambient Rhetoric- The Attunements of Rhetorical Being_(2013)_[My Copy].pdf",
      "page_start": 282,
      "page_end": 282,
      "text": "This moves me toward some final remarks. Dwelling on Toronto Island discloses the environment as a complex relational stitchwork of things individuating themselves, emerging from the as-a-whole into mutually conditioning and supporting aspects of being, including the human. The island is not nature. It is not separate from the human and hence in need of preservation from the ravages of human work and technology. Rather, the island is integral to the unique character of dwelling there. What Princen and the Toronto Islanders dance around, partially disclosing and not quite articulating, is that as a place that emerges through dwelling, the island gathers and is gathered by the fourfold. This occasions the strength of the residents' decades-long resistance to city planning and the lure of comfort and money. This is where the rejection of automobility emerges and draws its vigor. This is why the rejection is neither negative nor reactionary: it is the power of affirmation nourished in the \"as a whole\" of island life. My point is not that humans assign the values that some call \"uses\" but rather that the mutual conditioning of earth, sky, divinities, and mortals brings a haleness (which cannot be confused with utopian dreams, lack of struggle, etc., in some sort of easy holistic harmony) to \"their\" way of being, where \"their\" extends beyond mortals to include nonhuman elements.\n\nThis view offers a still greater understanding of the deficiency in Latour's discussion of the parliament of things. Latour's remarks suggest that addressing the Toronto Island conflict would be a mat ter of bringing all the elements of the island, considered as an assemblage of human and nonhuman actants, to political representation and deliberation. Such elements would include, presumably, the land, beaches, plants, animals, roads,\n\nbuildings, and everything else. Nor would Latour limit the considerations to distributed representation for things, for he extends the range of forums for interacting with things beyond the political to include religion, public discourse, the arts, science, and more. This remains an important point, since it grants value to disparate ways of dealing with things of concern. But Latour is less able to attend to the conditions of possibility for remaking the political into Dingpolitik. Everything is as it is, available for assembly and reassembly but still already there. Political assembly becomes a matter of human willing and pursuit, with the forums ready-made and rhetoric conceived as persuasion in its traditional sense, except that it now includes nonhuman actants and acknowledges their agency.\n\nHeidegger's insights remain more profound. His rejoinder to Latour would be that such political will and the rhetorics required to enact it ultimately reinforce the priority of the human. There is no fundamental tempering of the notion of will itself as it emerges in the assemblage and hence as it underpins Latour's conception ofrhetoric and politics; there are only the complications resulting from the establishment of nonhuman intermediaries. Reconceiving nonhumans as agents affecting even humans constitutes an advance, but Latour offers no larger sense of the \"as a whole\" within which humans, as well as everything else, find relation and place and that constitutes the possibility for everything's being what it is and for our being as we are. The examples Princen offers, including the Toronto Islanders as well as various European cities that have closed their downtown areas to autos, are remarkable because in each case, a way oflife enacts an attunement to this profound conditionality. 13 The important point here is that a Dingpolitik does not come first, opening up and transforming our existing political practices by an inclusive fiat extended to nonhumans. Rather, dwelling as attunement to this conditionality, disclosed in and through the fourfold, already gives rise to what can emerge as Dingpolitik.\n\nIfI may switch idioms here, Heidegger's argument ultimately resacralizes the world. The world has value, of a sort, and these values make claims on us as they become manifest through disclosive practices, the ensemble of which is a way of dwelling. I began the chapter on this note when I discussed Hyde's rethinking of ethos as dwelling. Such an ethos already includes ethical bearings, so that values emerge through everyday practice. As I have argued, from the perspective of an ambient rhetoric, these \"values\" transcend human being. In chapter 5 I discussed Heidegger's conception of freeing, and it comes up again here. The Toronto Island residents free their island to be what it is precisely by attending to the island as an\n\na",
      "meta": {
